@page "/learn/displayname-component"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>DisplayName · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">DisplayName</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your model already names its fields. Why does your grid type the names again?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Any render mode · about 4 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> <code>&lt;DisplayName For="..." /&gt;</code>, a component that shows a
        property's <code>[Display]</code> name on the page. In .NET 10, Blazor had nothing built in for that, so
        you typed the name again.
    </p>

    <p>Here's a supplier model. Each field gets its name from <code>[Display]</code>:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The model: each name is set once, here</span>
        <pre class="snip">public class SupplierForm
{
    [Display(Name = "Code", ResourceType = typeof(SupplierText))]
    public string? Code { get; set; }

    <span class="dim">// Company and Contact the same way</span>
}</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        With <code>ResourceType</code>, each <code>Name</code> is a key in a resource file, looked up in the
        visitor's language. None of this is new:
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The names in SupplierText.resx and SupplierText.de.resx</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">key        English          German</span>
Code       Supplier code    Lieferantencode
Company    Company name     Firmenname
Contact    Main contact     Hauptansprechpartner</pre>
    </div>
    <p>Now you want a grid of suppliers, with those names as its column headers. In .NET 10 you'd write this:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The .NET 10 grid</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;QuickGrid Items="suppliers"&gt;
    &lt;PropertyColumn <span class="err">Title="Supplier code"</span> Property="s =&gt; s.Code" /&gt;
    &lt;PropertyColumn <span class="err">Title="Company name"</span>  Property="s =&gt; s.Company" /&gt;
    &lt;PropertyColumn <span class="err">Title="Main contact"</span>  Property="s =&gt; s.Contact" /&gt;
&lt;/QuickGrid&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>The names are typed again, in English. Here it is running, for a visitor who reads German.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The .NET 10 grid" Url="demo/displayname/suppliers/net10?culture=de-DE"
                Label=".NET 10: headers typed as Title" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            The page is in German (<b>Lieferanten</b>), but the headers are still in English. The model has German
            names for all three. The grid never asks for them. Click <b>English</b> to compare.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The names live in two places</h2>
    <p>
        The model knows each field's name in every language. The grid doesn't ask it: it has its own English copy.
        So the headers can't follow the language, and if you rename a field on the model, the grid keeps the old
        name.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Ask the model for the name</h2>
    <p>Put a <code>&lt;DisplayName For&gt;</code> in each column's header:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The .NET 11 grid</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;PropertyColumn Property="s =&gt; s.Contact"&gt;
    &lt;HeaderTemplate&gt;<span class="hl">&lt;DisplayName For="() =&gt; form.Contact" /&gt;</span>&lt;/HeaderTemplate&gt;
&lt;/PropertyColumn&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>DisplayName</code> reads the <code>[Display]</code> name for <code>form.Contact</code> and shows it as
        plain text, in the current language. Here's the same grid with the fix on every column.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same grid, with DisplayName" Url="demo/displayname/suppliers?culture=de-DE"
                Label=".NET 11: DisplayName">
        <Caption>
            The same German page. This time the headers are in German too: <b>Lieferantencode</b>,
            <b>Firmenname</b>, <b>Hauptansprechpartner</b>. They come from the model. Click <b>English</b> and they
            switch back.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/DisplayNames/Suppliers.razor" Feature="displayname-component">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page in full. The grid's rows are <code>Supplier</code> database records, which have no
            <code>[Display]</code> names, so the headers point at the form's model instead: an empty
            <code>SupplierForm</code> field. <code>DisplayName</code> only needs to know which property you mean. It
            never reads the value, so the field can stay <code>null</code>.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Type the name, or write a helper</h2>
    <p>
        You could type the names, as Fig. 1 does, or write your own helper that reads <code>[Display]</code> with
        reflection and returns a string for <code>Title</code>. That works, but it's one more thing to write, test
        and copy between projects.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>One component, like MVC's DisplayNameFor</h2>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;DisplayName&gt;</code> is Blazor's version of MVC's <code>@@Html.DisplayNameFor</code>. It shows
        just the text, with no tag around it, so it works in a header, a table cell or a sentence.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>Why <code>HeaderTemplate</code>?</b> <code>Title</code> only takes a string, and <code>DisplayName</code> is a component.</li>
        <li><b>A <code>HeaderTemplate</code> drops QuickGrid's sort button.</b> On a sortable column, wrap the name in your own <code>&lt;button @@onclick="() =&gt; context.Grid.SortByColumnAsync(context)"&gt;</code>.</li>
        <li><b>It reads attributes, not <code>IStringLocalizer</code>.</b> To translate, use <code>[Display(ResourceType = ...)]</code>, as this model does.</li>
        <li><b>If there's no <code>[Display]</code></b>, it falls back to <code>[DisplayName]</code>, then to the property name.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
